00:10:59.922 --> 00:11:01.380
So the goal here
is that it's going

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.609
to be a fully managed
service, it has a rest API.

00:11:03.609 --> 00:11:06.150
The idea here is that you can
connect to it from other tools,

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:07.560
other CI systems.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.060
And then here it
also support things

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.600
like build triggers, tags,
or other custom build steps.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.730
And the idea here is that
it has full integration

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.560
into Google Container registry.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.200
Now this is the same service
that we use for App Engine

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.060
Flex, cloud functions.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:23.070
Those services use this
underneath the covers,

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:24.976
and you'll also
have access to it.

00:11:24.976 --> 00:11:26.850
And you also can use
your own custom scripts,

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:29.980
and we'll walk through
what that looks like here.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.452
So let's jump over to
the container builder.

00:11:32.452 --> 00:11:34.410
So now that we have this
binary up and running,

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.060
we can verify that it works.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.371
We want to build it now.

00:11:37.371 --> 00:11:39.120
OK so the first thing
you're going to need

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:44.280
is one of these Cloud
build like EML files.

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:47.075
So what we see here is
basically a list of containers.

00:11:47.075 --> 00:11:48.450
So you have a list
of steps, just

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:50.345
like any other build system.

00:11:50.345 --> 00:11:51.720
And the first
thing we want to do

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:54.210
is specify the
container we want to use

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.316
for this particular step.

00:11:55.316 --> 00:11:57.690
So in this case, I'm just
going to use one of the default

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.331
images from the Cloud Builder.

00:11:59.331 --> 00:11:59.830
Team.

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:02.775
So this has go link and it,
has the latest version of go,

00:12:02.775 --> 00:12:04.170
go1.8 is in there.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.450
So all I have to do is specify
the container I want to use.

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:11.190
That's going to mount my source
code into a workspace directory

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.526
and then run the
commands that I specify.

00:12:13.526 --> 00:12:15.900
So here I'm just going to say
go install, which will then

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:19.050
produce a binary that
will be available for me

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.250
in the workspace directory
for the next step.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:23.460
So now we kind of get
this pipeline thing going.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:24.918
And the next step
is I'm just going

00:12:24.918 --> 00:12:26.010
to use a Docker container.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:28.560
And what I want to do here
is just call Docker build.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.480
Now what this will
assume here is

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:34.040
that I have a Docker build
file in my workspace.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.940
So if I look at this
Docker build file,

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:38.590
you see here that I'm adding
the binary that comes out,

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:40.260
so it's out of my go path.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.600
There'll be this GIF maker
binary, I'm just copying it,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:43.933
and that's the only thing there.

00:12:43.933 --> 00:12:46.069
And I'm just using
Alpine as a base image.

00:12:46.069 --> 00:12:47.610
Some people will
say, hey why are you

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.261
not using the scratch image?

00:12:49.261 --> 00:12:51.510
Normally when I'm deploying
things like to production,

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.760
sometimes I do need to get
in there and troubleshoot.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.370
And not having some kind
of base image there the has

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.116
some utilities or the
ability to add utilities,

00:12:59.116 --> 00:13:00.990
makes it a little bit
harder to troubleshoot,

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:02.670
especially if you're
in production.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.160
So I usually use a very small,
minimal image like this.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.730
So given that if we
were to push this,

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:12.480
we should create this particular
image from our source code.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.290
So we'll run that command now.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:18.480
Now I'm going to cheat
and rely on my history

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.070
to know what command that is.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.380
So what we want to do
here is take that config,

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:24.310
take our current
context, and push it up

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:27.250
to container builder.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:32.000
I don't want to know your
data plan is going to be but,

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.715
it's going to be a
little expensive.

00:13:34.715 --> 00:13:36.090
All right so what
we're doing now

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.090
is tying up all the
content, so we're pushing it

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:40.170
to container builder.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.590
And this is actually a
pretty good idea because now

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:45.500
that we're not pulling down
all those Docker containers

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.750
on my laptop, because
your data plan would

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:50.630
be just totally screwed
if I did that, we're

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:52.880
doing all this on the other
side, on the server side.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:55.160
So all the logs here are
available as Stackdriver

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:56.100
logging.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.210
So what you have here now is all
the steps are coming out here.

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:01.310
And if everything works,
what you'll see is we'll

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:04.400
have our resulting image
pushed to container registry

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:05.450
automatically.

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:08.000
Now the tool is smart enough
to use your service credentials

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.125
or anything that you have
set up in IM so it pushes

00:14:10.125 --> 00:14:12.410
to the right place,
and once it's there,

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:15.370
it's available for me to use.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:18.760
Now if I were to pop over
to the container builder

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:22.090
UI, which is part of the
container registry UI,

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:23.610
we click over here.

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:25.480
And we also have all
your build history,

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:28.595
so here you can kind of
drill in to all of your logs

00:14:28.595 --> 00:14:29.470
to see what happened.

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:32.245
You can go into each
individual step if you want to.

00:14:32.245 --> 00:14:34.120
And this is where you
can kind of log and see

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:35.860
things that are working.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.410
And now that we have this
particular image available,

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:41.430
we can look at our
container registry

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:43.720
and we'll see that the
image should be there.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:45.330
So let's find it here.

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:46.740
GIF maker.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:48.620
And we'll see that
it's tagged there.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:51.800
So at this point, we should be
able to push our application

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.860
to our development environment.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:59.570
So let's talk a little bit
about the registry before we do.

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:02.000
So the container registry,
the goal of this,

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.940
and why did we build this, why
not just use the Docker hub?

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.670
Well number one, people
want their content images

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.370
to be accessible
and make them fast.

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:11.930
So having Google
container registry

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:15.770
means that your images are
stored inside the same cloud

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:16.730
infrastructure.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:18.320
It's backed by
Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.099
so all your images
are replicated,

00:15:20.099 --> 00:15:21.890
you don't have to worry
about those things,

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:25.372
and it's private by default. So
it has integration to IAM, so

00:15:25.372 --> 00:15:27.830
all the things you're using
for the rest of your resources,

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:31.164
we piggyback on top
of those things.

00:15:31.164 --> 00:15:32.580
Some of the other
things, the team

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:34.900
is really up to date on the
image format we support.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:37.830
So Docker v2 image
format and SOCI

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.320
continues to make progress,
that's the open container image

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:43.170
format, we'll have
support for that too.

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:45.010
Vulnerability scanning
will be built in.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.135
So if you look at some of
the command line tooling,

00:15:47.135 --> 00:15:49.290
you start to see hints
of that in our UI.

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:51.816
And the goal here is that
we'll start by notifying you

00:15:51.816 --> 00:15:53.940
if there's any vulnerabilities
found in your image,

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:55.671
maybe inside of your base image.

00:15:55.671 --> 00:15:58.170
Tools will get more advanced
and start doing static analysis

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:00.630
to tell you, hey, you're
using a vulnerable library,

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.270
you might want to update
that and do a rebuild.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.700
And it's backed by Google Cloud
storage like we saw before.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:10.032
Now when you're using this, you
can definitely use the command

00:16:10.032 --> 00:16:10.990
line to do most things.

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:15.321
So you can list tags
for all your images,

00:16:15.321 --> 00:16:17.320
and here's where you would
see any vulnerability

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:18.620
reports that show up.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:21.160
So here's our first
image that we pushed.

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.389
You can list all the
images you have there.

00:16:23.389 --> 00:16:25.180
Even though this seems
like a simple thing,

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:27.340
most container registries
do not have a way for you

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:29.756
to figure out what the tags
are, you have to go to the GUI

00:16:29.756 --> 00:16:30.760
and drill down.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:33.310
So having this on the command
line is super easy for me

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:34.934
to figure out which
I want to play with

00:16:34.934 --> 00:16:36.160
and what I want to deploy.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:39.190
So all this is kind of built
in, all this is integrated.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.680
So so far we've talked about
building a cloud native

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:44.680
app, this idea that
we want to build it,

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:47.470
but we don't want to build
things from our laptop,

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:49.530
we want something
like a build trigger.

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:52.150
OK, so now that I have this
application, what I really

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:54.250
want to do is set
up a build trigger

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:56.290
that will automatically
build this app,

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:57.650
but not on every check in.

00:16:57.650 --> 00:16:59.620
I actually experimented
with having a container

00:16:59.620 --> 00:17:00.940
built on every check in.

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:05.079
How many people have
run out of space before?

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:06.369
That's what that feels like.

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:07.150
OK?

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.069
So what I found
works a little bit

00:17:09.069 --> 00:17:11.990
better is build the
containers on tags.

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:13.810
So when I tag something,
that's when I want

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:15.619
my build to actually happen.

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:18.819
So here I'll go to my build
triggers, and I can define one.

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:21.750
So here's a build trigger,
and the UI is pretty nice,

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:25.030
you can go through and
pick particular hosting

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.630
repositories, like GitHub.

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:29.920
And here, I want to have
a tag trigger a build,

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.980
and when that tag shows up
matching my regular expression,

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:35.800
I want it to run the commands
in the cloudbuild.yaml.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:38.650
So we need to make one
modification for this

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:39.860
to actually work.

00:17:39.860 --> 00:17:47.260
So we want to, instead of hard
coding the tag number there,

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:52.870
we want to actually have it
replaced with the actual tag

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:54.340
on the repository.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:59.470
So I'll just do like that
whole cooking show thing

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:01.600
and then let's take a look.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.440
So we'll grab the tag name
and make that our image name

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:06.420
and have it trigger
the build that way.

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:10.180
So we'll push this up
and then we'll tag it.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:13.285
So here we'll just
do git status.

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:17.330
Man, look at all
that other stuff.

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:19.580
I don't know if I
should check it in.

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:24.110
How many people just do git
add don't even look at it

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:25.880
You're that guy like,
hey man, who checked

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:27.400
in their PlayStation games?

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:30.470
Like, oh, my bad dude.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.840
I'll fix it.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:37.550
All right so what we'll
do here is, who cares.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:40.200
I think we just add it all.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.910
I really want to
see what that is.

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:46.380
That looks safe, I think.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:47.956
All right, let's just do that.

00:18:47.956 --> 00:18:49.330
So let's just do
this thing here.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:53.950
So what you should do always
is just blindly do that.

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:56.360
And then the worst thing
is when people do this.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:57.685
Updating stuff.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:04.890
It's like, dude, how am I
going to co-review that?

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:07.900
Yeah you updated stuff,
but it's not right.

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:09.850
Just take it as is, dude.

00:19:09.850 --> 00:19:12.470
All right, so now we
have this new thing up.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:13.720
Ideally it should still build.

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.750
So the next thing you want
to do is tag it, so git tag.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:20.650
And then we'll call
it 0.0.1 And then

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:28.521
we'll say git
push, origin, tags.

00:19:28.521 --> 00:19:29.020
What?

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:29.519
Oh.

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:36.950
All right, so we
push this there.

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:39.050
We should trigger a build.

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:42.316
Please beta, be working.

00:19:42.316 --> 00:19:43.940
So the first warning
is when you log in

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.511
and it says this at the top.

00:19:46.511 --> 00:19:50.160
You need to think about
that a little bit.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:51.290
So we come over here.

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:52.560
Uh-oh.

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.890
A build is triggering.

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:57.355
Do we get some logs?

00:19:57.355 --> 00:19:59.980
I'm so happy on the inside right
now, you guys don't even know.

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:10.640
It's fast, too.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:14.300
So now we have the 1.0 image
based on the build trigger.

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:18.020
So now my workflow could
be, build my application,

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:19.160
test locally--

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:20.800
you might want to answer that.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:24.020
Ask them if they have
any internet access.

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:27.230
Push it locally, and then
once I tag the repository,

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:29.460
I now have this automatic
bill trigger that I

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:30.680
don't have to think about.

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.244
So all of this is
kind of built in,

00:20:32.244 --> 00:20:34.160
so automatically in
Google Container registry,

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.390
so at this point, I'm ready
to start consuming that image

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:40.490
inside of my container cluster.

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:43.130
All right, so we have our
build trigger in place,

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:44.790
so let's move on
to the next step.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:47.300
So this is kind of what we want
out of this registry system.

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:48.620
We want to be fully automated.

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.080
And again, the design here
is that you can hook up

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:54.000
other tools to do this as well.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:55.870
All right, this guy's
still working out,

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:57.790
he's probably tired.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:21:00.280
So I want to talking about
Google Container Engine.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:04.650
If you haven't used GKE, the
best way to think about it

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:06.180
is we take open-source
Kubernetes.

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.520
We don't modify it
in any way, but we

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:11.820
do glue in all the
Google services,

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:15.886
like the logging from
Stackdriver, logging

00:21:15.886 --> 00:21:18.010
for Stackdriver, we do
monitoring from Stackdriver,

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:20.340
we pull the metrics
from the cluster itself.

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:23.160
We take care of all the
upgrades and scaling

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:25.440
the nose if you
turn on that option.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:28.050
One of the best features I'd
like is that IAM integration.

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:31.320
So anything that you use from
QCTL from your credentials,

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:34.140
once we have the
detailed R back,

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:37.200
that will be integrated into
the alpha announcement we

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:39.270
had today with custom IM roles.

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:41.730
You'll be able to map that
to your Kubernetes roles

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:43.505
and be able to control access.

00:21:43.505 --> 00:21:45.630
And then it's also easy to
manage multiple clusters

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:46.680
at a time.

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:50.400
Now the reason for
this is we want

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:53.130
you to focus on using
Kubernetes and not managing it.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:55.020
Now having all of
this integration also

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:58.270
leads to the next
thing, which is managing

00:21:58.270 --> 00:21:59.940
development environments.

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.370
Now this is where it gets real
confusing for most people.

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:03.510
The first thing
people do when they

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:06.093
get a Kubernetes cluster is give
everyone in the company QCTL.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:12.390
This guy's laughing his
ass off becuase that

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:14.339
leads to a bunch of problems.

00:22:14.339 --> 00:22:16.380
Number one by default,
most people are giving out

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:19.590
admin credentials, so
people are adding things,

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:21.497
deleting things that
they shouldn't delete.

00:22:21.497 --> 00:22:23.580
So we've got to talk about
a better way of sharing

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:24.820
a cluster in development.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:26.790
And I do like the
idea of self-service.

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:29.220
You do not want to operate
on a ticketing system

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.530
to get someone to deploy
something in Kubernetes.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:34.200
We made it far too easy to
use the cluster to do that.

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:36.660
So if you're in OPS, the
way you think about this

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:39.540
is to provide a sandbox
for testing, and playing

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:41.982
with these configs until they
get it right in production.

00:22:41.982 --> 00:22:44.440
So what we want to do is talk
about how to share a cluster.

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:47.100
Now I think name spaces
is one of the best ways

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:49.380
to share a cluster
inside of Kubernetes.

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:51.750
You can give a namespace
to a whole team,

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:54.090
you can give a namespace
to individual developers

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:56.790
and let them go wild
in that little sandbox.

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:58.740
But the key, once you
issue that namespace,

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.220
is you want to use quotas
to limit what they can use

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:03.751
in that particular namespace.

00:23:03.751 --> 00:23:05.250
You want to prevent
mistakes, right.

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:07.230
We're not trying to defend
against malicious users

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.313
but we want to make sure
people don't accidentally

00:23:09.313 --> 00:23:12.552
consume too much and give
direct access to this.

00:23:12.552 --> 00:23:14.010
So if you haven't
used name spaces,

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:16.010
we're going to flip over
and show you how people

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:17.430
are managing these things.

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:19.941
So one thing people
are doing is they

00:23:19.941 --> 00:23:22.440
have a git repository that holds
a lot of the infrastructure

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:23.114
stuff.

00:23:23.114 --> 00:23:24.780
If you come from a
infrastructurist code

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:25.950
background, you're
kind of familiar

00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:27.200
with this particular model.

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:29.850
Here, this is where
restoring our configfs.

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:33.930
So what we want to do here
is we'll have a dev setup.

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:38.550
So as a cluster administrator,
I want to prep these name spaces

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:40.560
and put quotas in place.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:43.680
As a developer, I'm
assuming my credentials

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:47.250
that are given to me are scoped
to that particular namespace,

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.770
and I can just push
that namespace and deal

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:51.250
with the resources that I have.

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.250
Now one thing is important
you have to remember,

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:55.320
the name space and
a single cluster

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:57.026
is shared across all the nodes.

00:23:57.026 --> 00:23:58.400
So you're not
going to limit them

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:00.457
on what nodes their
workloads can land on,

00:24:00.457 --> 00:24:02.040
so you want to put
that quota in place

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:04.377
to make sure that you're
sharing resources effectively.

00:24:04.377 --> 00:24:06.210
So the first thing I
want to do is make sure

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:07.870
that I'm using
the right cluster.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:11.310
So for those that don't know
kubectl, the Kubernetes command

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:13.326
line tool, supports
multiple clusters,

00:24:13.326 --> 00:24:14.700
you can just squish
between them.

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:19.650
So here we're going to use
kubectl, config, use-context,

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:21.960
and then we're going to say dev.

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:24.370
So now I'm pointing
to my dev cluster.

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:26.530
And here I'm going to
create a namespace.

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:30.649
There's this really annoying
developer hightower, this guy,

00:24:30.649 --> 00:24:32.940
and he's just rambunctious,
thinks he knows everything.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:34.773
So I'm going to give
him his own name space,

00:24:34.773 --> 00:24:37.980
and he can go wild in
that particular namespace.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:42.670
So as a cluster administrator,
I'll say kubectl apply,

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:44.920
and I'll create
this new namespace.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:46.630
So once this
namespace is in place,

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:48.370
before I hand out
credentials, I'm

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:52.000
also going to put a
quota in place here.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:57.231
All right, so this quota
will allow only one service.

00:24:57.231 --> 00:24:59.230
Probably one of the most
important quota things,

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:00.770
people start creating
a bunch of services,

00:25:00.770 --> 00:25:02.170
you're going to have
a bunch of public IPs

00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:04.544
and you're going to be spending
a lot of money at Google.

00:25:04.544 --> 00:25:07.430
That's probably a good idea,
so probably up that number

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:08.607
if you want to.

00:25:08.607 --> 00:25:10.190
We're going to limit
it to three pods,

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:12.160
and then we're going to
limit the amount of CPU

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:13.910
and other resources
that they can require.

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:18.160
So here, we're just going
to do an apply here as well.

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:20.340
All right so now our
namespace is set up.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:23.370
Now we're able to give our
credentials to the developer.

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:26.100
So what we want to do here
is limit what we give them

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:28.030
by default. I'm just
going to switch contexts,

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:30.000
but when you give
out your configs,

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.460
you can actually put them
in a specific context,

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:33.990
give them a
namespace, so it just

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:35.266
defaults to the right thing.

00:25:35.266 --> 00:25:37.140
Right, and then maybe
limit their credentials

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:38.880
on what they can switch to.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:43.710
So here we'll say kubectl,
config, use-context.

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:46.720
And here I'll just use
the khightower context.

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:49.050
All right, so once I'm in
the khightower context,

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:51.750
I now have my own environment
and sandbox to play in.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:55.570
So I say kubectl
get pods, hopefully

00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:56.740
there's nothing running.

00:25:56.740 --> 00:25:58.364
And then also we can
look at our quota.

00:25:58.364 --> 00:26:00.305
So kubectl describe quota.

00:26:03.050 --> 00:26:06.290
And we can see the limits
that are put in place.

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:09.330
So at this point, that's
all I have to work with.

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:11.090
So I'm going to deploy
that application

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:12.252
that we had earlier.

00:26:12.252 --> 00:26:14.210
So here I'm just going
to update my deployment.

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:17.690
So in my Dev, I check
in my Kubernetes configs

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:18.969
next to my application.

00:26:18.969 --> 00:26:21.260
If you're running things like
mini cube on your laptop,

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:23.810
it will give you a one
node Kubernetes cluster.

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:25.700
It's a good idea to
test out these configfs

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:28.370
before you push them up and
push them to other higher

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:29.850
level environments.

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:32.690
So here, what I'm going to do
is look at my Kubernetes config

00:26:32.690 --> 00:26:34.760
for my deployment object.

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:38.840
And you'll notice a few
things here for configuration.

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:42.320
Here you can see that
I'm pointing at the image

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:45.100
that we just built
from that tag.

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:47.650
Down here, I'm having
my configs separated.

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:49.810
A lot of people struggle
with this idea of where do

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:51.790
I put my configuration files?

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:55.030
I recommend you do not put
them inside of your image.

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:57.880
Do not build a new image to
deploy to an environment that

00:26:57.880 --> 00:26:59.270
has your configs in it.

00:26:59.270 --> 00:27:01.120
Kubernetes makes it
too easy for you just

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:03.430
to have your configs
parked outside.

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:06.340
Inside of Kubernetes, in this
case, we're using config maps.

00:27:06.340 --> 00:27:07.810
Here we're specifying
the database

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.480
we want to connect to,
the bucket we want to use,

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:12.820
and the project ID that
we need to consume.

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:15.160
Now my application
doesn't necessarily

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:17.410
take environment
variables, so I'm

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:20.830
able to reference those
config maps in my flags

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:22.330
by using this method
here where you

00:27:22.330 --> 00:27:24.252
can refer to
environment variables

00:27:24.252 --> 00:27:26.710
and Kubernetes will substitute
them before they're actually

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:27.555
deployed.

00:27:27.555 --> 00:27:28.930
Now here's the
other bit that you

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:30.970
need to think about when you're
managing these environments.

00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:32.710
Even in Dev and
production, you want

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:34.270
to know how your
application performs

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:35.960
with these kind of constraints.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.001
When I talk to customers,
this is the first thing

00:27:38.001 --> 00:27:39.070
that they fall down on.

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:41.444
Most people are used to having
the whole VM or bare metal

00:27:41.444 --> 00:27:43.600
machine at their disposal
and they've never

00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:45.259
been capped before.

00:27:45.259 --> 00:27:47.800
You take that application and
you move it into an environment

00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:49.500
where we start
enforcing cgroups,

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:51.300
it starts behaving weird, right?

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:53.190
They just say the
app is slow now.

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:55.090
Right, of course it
is, we're throttling

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:56.617
how much CPU you can use.

00:27:56.617 --> 00:27:58.450
So you need to start
measuring these things,

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.200
I would say even at
the lower environments

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:01.640
or even on your laptop.

00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:05.540
So make sure you put in the
limits and request in place.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:08.090
And here we're mounting
in our config maps.

00:28:08.090 --> 00:28:11.504
Also my service
account that I need

00:28:11.504 --> 00:28:12.920
to connect to all
those resources,

00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:14.726
just like we saw on
the command line.

00:28:14.726 --> 00:28:16.850
And the other thing that
I do in my Dev environment

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:21.410
is I also check in my
configuration options.

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:23.952
So if you look here, I'm going
to connect the Dev bucket

00:28:23.952 --> 00:28:25.910
because that's the thing
that I have access to.

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:29.090
I'm going to connect to the
Dev database inside of Spanner.

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:32.780
I'm also going to use my
personal project ID, right?

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:34.370
So as a developer,
I know I'm pointing

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:35.480
to the right namespace.

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:39.489
So what we'll do here,
say kubectl apply dash f.

00:28:39.489 --> 00:28:41.780
And then I'm going to say
this whole Kubernetes folder.

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:43.430
So most people don't know,
you can actually just

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:45.790
point to a whole folder and
it'll take all the configs

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:47.850
and push them to Kubernetes
at the same time,

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:50.280
so I don't have to
do them one by one.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:52.860
Now if I push this
up, we should now

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:56.970
have our configuration files
and credentials all in place,

00:28:56.970 --> 00:28:58.227
and our deployment there.

00:28:58.227 --> 00:28:59.810
So let's see if it's
actually running.

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:08.714
It is running, as I expect
it with lots of confidence.

00:29:08.714 --> 00:29:10.630
So with this running, I
can now connect to it.

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:16.051
So beforehand, I created a
service, kubectl, get svc,

00:29:16.051 --> 00:29:17.800
and then we can actually
look at our quota

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:21.350
again to see how much
we're actually taking up.

00:29:21.350 --> 00:29:24.440
So that actually cost me
about half of my CPU limit,

00:29:24.440 --> 00:29:26.780
so I'm not going to
deploy much more here.

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:29.660
So what happens if you
do accidentally try

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:31.520
to scale this out a little bit?

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:33.770
So let's try it really
quick, so kubectl.

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:37.450
We're going to scale
the deployment object.

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:38.980
So I'm going to
scale my deployment

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:40.320
inside of Kubernetes.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:45.142
And this is going to be called
GIF maker, replicas equal 3.

00:29:45.142 --> 00:29:47.600
And then what we'll do is we'll
also watch for some events,

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:49.650
so we actually see what's
going on in Kubernetes.

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:54.990
Kubectl, get events, --watch.

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:57.660
So here's all the events that
are coming out of our cluster.

00:29:57.660 --> 00:29:58.890
So we're about to
kick off this command

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:00.390
and we're going to
see what happens.

00:30:00.390 --> 00:30:01.960
You want to observe
the behavior.

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:05.420
So I'm going to ask
for three of these.

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:08.970
And as I asked for three of
these, we get some errors here.

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:09.550
Right?

00:30:09.550 --> 00:30:11.040
So we'll cut this.

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:13.470
And we'll see that some of
them fail because we actually

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:14.970
hit our quota.

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:17.100
So we're going to end
up with this only two

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:18.540
out of three pods.

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:21.290
And the deployment
controller behind the scenes

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:24.930
will continue to try to
create the third pod, hoping

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:26.825
that quota will show up
to allow us to do it.

00:30:26.825 --> 00:30:28.200
But in the meanwhile,
we're going

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:30.270
to continue to hit
this error and we

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:32.490
won't be able to
actually use any more

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:33.920
inside this
particular namespace.

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:37.710
If I say kubctl get pods,
we'll only have two running.

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:41.340
If I look at my quota, we can
see that I'm out of quota,

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:43.470
and at this point we
can have a conversation

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:45.570
with the administrator,
or you can

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:47.730
be monitoring the events
that are coming out

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:51.060
of Kubernetes to say, hey, we
have a namespace that seems

00:30:51.060 --> 00:30:53.040
to be needing more resources.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:55.290
And if the cluster
isn't fully utilized,

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:59.160
you can come back here and add
resources to that particular

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.540
quota,or to that
particular cluster.

00:31:01.540 --> 00:31:03.327
So we can bump that
up a little bit.

00:31:03.327 --> 00:31:05.160
It takes a moment for
this thing to resolve,

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:06.750
but we can go ahead
and do it now.

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:09.300
So we'll say kubectl
compute resources.

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.620
And we'll bump some of
this up, we;ll say, hey,

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:14.670
let's go ahead and
give you four here,

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:18.680
we'll give you eight
here, and those are fine.

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:21.070
And then we'll
say kubectl apply.

00:31:21.070 --> 00:31:22.780
So this is me as a
cluster administrator,

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:25.030
I'm noticing that there
needs to be something fixed.

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:27.880
Apply-f, compute resources.

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:33.020
So at this point,
there's more quota inside

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:35.660
of that particular namespace.

00:31:35.660 --> 00:31:37.610
We see the limits
have been risen.

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:39.470
So at this point, the
deployment controller

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:41.990
will try and try
until it finds room

00:31:41.990 --> 00:31:43.730
to actually run this workload.

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:46.100
So let's see what's happening.

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:49.225
Kubectl get pods.

00:31:49.225 --> 00:31:51.700
All right, so this
will take a moment.

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:54.380
And then when it realizes that
it will work, it will show up.

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:56.796
So we're just going to let
that continue in the background

00:31:56.796 --> 00:31:58.490
as we move on to production.

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:00.430
So we have one
more main use case

00:32:00.430 --> 00:32:02.680
that I want to show you
when dealing with this,

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:05.140
and also how container
builder can help us even

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:06.842
for the production situation.

00:32:06.842 --> 00:32:08.050
So look at this really quick.

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:13.670
This guy's still
doing his thing.

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:16.697
So for production environments.

00:32:16.697 --> 00:32:19.280
This is where I think you should
just use a dedicated cluster.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.740
One, you don't want to
upgrade Kubernetes and try out

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:22.730
new features.

00:32:22.730 --> 00:32:24.500
And the last thing
you want to do

00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:26.090
is upgrade Kubernetes
because you

00:32:26.090 --> 00:32:29.330
want to use some new feature
for Dev, to experiment

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:30.980
and take down production.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:31.760
All right?

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:34.460
You do not want to fill
out that post mortem.

00:32:34.460 --> 00:32:36.080
There's no reason to.

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:37.640
The idea here is
that the configs

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:39.320
you write in one
environment ideally

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:40.730
can move to the
next environment,

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:42.350
because all you
have to change is

00:32:42.350 --> 00:32:45.560
the config settings between
different clusters or name

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:46.820
spaces.

00:32:46.820 --> 00:32:48.655
And we saw the resource
quotas and limits.

00:32:48.655 --> 00:32:50.030
There's another
thing that I want

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:51.415
to show you that I actually
don't have on a slide

00:32:51.415 --> 00:32:53.706
that I think is important,
is like you want real health

00:32:53.706 --> 00:32:54.320
checks.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:57.300
So when we look at our
production environments,

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:01.220
and we'll go over here,
we want to make sure

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:05.364
